# The public CI runner's CPU count, which every CI run copies: Jenkins (the maintainer's private repo), GitHub Actions and any
# agent's `scripts/ci/test.sh`.
#
# CI_RUNNER_CPUS: GitHub's ubuntu-latest runner for a public repository — 4 vCPUs (its log: "parallel: … (4 CPUs)").
# Every ruby / ruby-pg / frontend / flake container gets that much CPU TIME (docker --cpus N: a CFS quota, no fixed
# cores — Jenkins runs many builds at once, and a fixed --cpuset-cpus would put them all on the same N cores), and every
# CPU-sized worker count is N (vitest --maxWorkers, the flake waves). What it matches is CONTENTION — N CPUs' worth of
# work at once — not a shared VM's slower cores, and it cannot reproduce a noisy neighbour's stall (150 pinned
# runs never hit the 2 s stall that reddened a run on GitHub). The guard against stalls is the clock rule: code reads
# the time only through Busyyaks::Clock and tests stub it, never race it (test/unit/no_real_clock_test.rb).
CI_RUNNER_CPUS=4
# CI_FLAKE_REPEATS: how many times the flake stage runs each test file the change touches, on those CPUs, at most
# CI_RUNNER_CPUS runs at a time (the repeats in waves): all at once ran 265 Ruby processes beside the two suites and hit
# the Jenkins agent's TasksMax ("can't create Thread: Resource temporarily unavailable").
CI_FLAKE_REPEATS=5
